sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2019-boundary-13> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Memory can be the beginning of modularity because learning compresses complex states into simple triggers; in somatic control networks this appears as developmental modules in which a single simple driver (e.g. a bioelectric state) initiates a complex morphogenetic cascade such as building an eye or hand.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Memory and learning are the origin of developmental modularity";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Levin M, “The Computational Boundary of a \"Self\": Developmental Bioelectricity Drives Multicellularity and Scale-Free Cognition”, Frontiers in psychology (2019), doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2019.02688; section “The Agent’s Evolutionary Back-Story: Scaling of Information by Bioelectricity”. Scope: Developmental control networks in animals"
}
